WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:02.220
-: Before we talk about the ways we can use git diff

00:02.220 --> 00:04.350
the different variations, the options,

00:04.350 --> 00:06.360
let's discuss the output.

00:06.360 --> 00:08.460
So, the output that we get from git diff,

00:08.460 --> 00:11.730
whatever the diff is that we're trying to generate,

00:11.730 --> 00:14.340
it always follows a very similar pattern.

00:14.340 --> 00:16.290
And it looks something like this.

00:16.290 --> 00:17.370
You can see over here on the right,

00:17.370 --> 00:18.960
it's kind of crazy looking.

00:18.960 --> 00:21.960
I mean, we see hopefully, relatively clearly,

00:21.960 --> 00:24.480
some of the code or changes in a file.

00:24.480 --> 00:26.340
But then there's a bunch of stuff before.

00:26.340 --> 00:27.570
We've got pluses.

00:27.570 --> 00:29.310
We also have minuses.

00:29.310 --> 00:32.730
We have weird looking symbols, these "@@s".

00:32.730 --> 00:35.640
So, let's walk through what exactly is going on.

00:35.640 --> 00:39.000
So for these diagrams, I'm using a very simple example

00:39.000 --> 00:42.630
of a repository, where we have a file called "rainbow.txt".

00:42.630 --> 00:43.770
And I made a commit.

00:43.770 --> 00:45.090
It looked like this,

00:45.090 --> 00:46.800
red, orange, yellow, green, blue, purple.

00:46.800 --> 00:48.930
That's how I learned the rainbow when I was a little kid.

00:48.930 --> 00:50.970
It was just blue and purple at the end.

00:50.970 --> 00:53.580
But then, we make some new changes.

00:53.580 --> 00:55.650
We haven't committed anything, but I made some changes

00:55.650 --> 00:57.900
in the working directory in the same file.

00:57.900 --> 01:02.310
And I updated it to be Blue indigo Violet, ROYGBIV.

01:02.310 --> 01:03.143
Okay.

01:03.143 --> 01:05.580
So, clearly we can see it's a a minor difference.

01:05.580 --> 01:08.820
We changed the same file so that purple is gone.

01:08.820 --> 01:11.850
We deleted that, and we added indigo and violet.

01:11.850 --> 01:14.100
So, when I run the git diff command,

01:14.100 --> 01:16.350
and again, I'll walk through doing that

01:16.350 --> 01:17.730
and the different variations.

01:17.730 --> 01:21.180
But I'll get an output that looks something like this.

01:21.180 --> 01:22.860
So, there's a lot of stuff going on,

01:22.860 --> 01:25.800
even just for that tiny little bit of a change.

01:25.800 --> 01:27.360
Hopefully, you can see, right?

01:27.360 --> 01:29.640
We see purple in red with a minus sign,

01:29.640 --> 01:32.640
indigo and violet in green with a plus sign.

01:32.640 --> 01:34.500
But let's talk about what we actually see

01:34.500 --> 01:36.690
on this diff, on this output.

01:36.690 --> 01:39.690
So the first thing up top, it's gonna say "diff"

01:39.690 --> 01:41.190
and then "--git"

01:41.190 --> 01:42.810
But then it's going to have the files

01:42.810 --> 01:44.550
that are being compared.

01:44.550 --> 01:47.550
Usually, these are actually going to be the same file

01:47.550 --> 01:50.160
just two versions, the old version of Rainbow,

01:50.160 --> 01:51.570
and the new version, right?

01:51.570 --> 01:53.730
The version from the last commit

01:53.730 --> 01:55.650
and the version in my working directory.

01:55.650 --> 01:57.690
But it could be different files.

01:57.690 --> 01:59.550
You would have to set that up yourself.

01:59.550 --> 02:01.740
Typically, it's the same file

02:01.740 --> 02:03.030
just over time.

02:03.030 --> 02:05.313
Between commits, between branches,

02:05.313 --> 02:08.340
between your staging area and your working directory.

02:08.340 --> 02:09.990
It's the same file, typically.

02:09.990 --> 02:14.010
So, Git is going to call one A and the other one B.

02:14.010 --> 02:15.930
So, you can see "rainbow.txt".

02:15.930 --> 02:18.157
The first one here is going to be A.

02:18.157 --> 02:20.910
"rainbow.txt", the second one will be B.

02:20.910 --> 02:24.750
So, we may get an output that's a lot more complicated.

02:24.750 --> 02:27.720
But there's always going to be the first line

02:27.720 --> 02:30.270
the files that are being compared, two files.

02:30.270 --> 02:33.327
So, "helpers.scss" compared to "helpers.scss".

02:34.440 --> 02:36.630
And here's what changed in that file.

02:36.630 --> 02:38.257
And, here's another file.

02:38.257 --> 02:39.517
"main.css"

02:39.517 --> 02:41.340
"main.css".

02:41.340 --> 02:44.460
So, if I just rerun the command that I had earlier,

02:44.460 --> 02:47.430
we can see, it's telling me here is the diff

02:47.430 --> 02:50.760
between index html and index html.

02:50.760 --> 02:52.620
And then, I keep going down.

02:52.620 --> 02:53.857
Now, we have a different file,

02:53.857 --> 02:56.700
"grid.js" and "grid.js".

02:56.700 --> 02:58.807
If I keep going down, here's another file.

02:58.807 --> 02:59.947
"main.css",

02:59.947 --> 03:01.920
"main.css".

03:01.920 --> 03:02.753
Okay?

03:02.753 --> 03:04.156
So, it's just telling us the two files,

03:04.156 --> 03:07.500
usually, they're the same file over time.

03:07.500 --> 03:10.140
Then, the next line does not matter for the most part.

03:10.140 --> 03:14.280
I've never needed to ever take a look or understand it.

03:14.280 --> 03:17.670
It's metadata about the files that are being compared.

03:17.670 --> 03:19.710
Each file actually gets its own hash.

03:19.710 --> 03:21.210
That's what we're seeing here.

03:21.210 --> 03:22.830
But really, it doesn't matter.

03:22.830 --> 03:25.650
And then, this is an internal file mode identifier.

03:25.650 --> 03:27.600
Again, not important.

03:27.600 --> 03:30.300
Then we see two lines of markers.

03:30.300 --> 03:33.780
This is gits way of telling us that for file A,

03:33.780 --> 03:36.900
whatever that is, it's going to be indicated changes

03:36.900 --> 03:39.930
in file A will be indicated with a minus sign.

03:39.930 --> 03:42.060
And file B will be a plus sign.

03:42.060 --> 03:44.253
So, that part's hopefully straightforward.

03:44.253 --> 03:47.730
Then, we get to chunks.

03:47.730 --> 03:50.820
So, you might have a file that's 10,000 lines long.

03:50.820 --> 03:52.260
You changed one line.

03:52.260 --> 03:55.530
Git diff is not gonna show you the entire 10,000 lines.

03:55.530 --> 03:57.780
It's going to show you the change,

03:57.780 --> 03:59.100
that part that was changed.

03:59.100 --> 04:02.640
And then a little bit of context before and after.

04:02.640 --> 04:04.890
So we can see, you know, here's what was changed,

04:04.890 --> 04:06.900
and then there's some context before.

04:06.900 --> 04:08.010
And it's at the end of the file,

04:08.010 --> 04:09.630
so there's nothing after.

04:09.630 --> 04:12.600
Here, I changed this line here.

04:12.600 --> 04:15.210
So, we get a little bit of context before

04:15.210 --> 04:16.650
and then some after.

04:16.650 --> 04:18.090
And then here's another change.

04:18.090 --> 04:20.700
We got context before and after.

04:20.700 --> 04:22.860
So, this file itself might have been

04:22.860 --> 04:25.020
a couple hundred lines, I think it is,

04:25.020 --> 04:27.120
but it's only showing us a small selection,

04:27.120 --> 04:29.790
a small chunk around each change.

04:29.790 --> 04:32.650
So, each chunk starts with a chunk header

04:33.570 --> 04:34.403
which looks like,

04:34.403 --> 04:36.510
oh, here's just multiple chunks by the way.

04:36.510 --> 04:39.690
This is one file, "main.css".

04:39.690 --> 04:42.600
It has one chunk and then a second chunk,

04:42.600 --> 04:45.810
just two different areas in the file that were changed.

04:45.810 --> 04:48.030
So, a chunk header is the header at the beginning

04:48.030 --> 04:49.800
of each chunk, and you can identify them

04:49.800 --> 04:53.310
by the two "@" signs on either side.

04:53.310 --> 04:55.680
And then some weird looking numbers inside.

04:55.680 --> 04:56.513
All right?

04:56.513 --> 04:58.800
So, there are two pairs of numbers.

04:58.800 --> 05:02.130
One set of these numbers corresponds to file A,

05:02.130 --> 05:03.960
the other file B.

05:03.960 --> 05:06.630
That's what the minus sign here indicates.

05:06.630 --> 05:10.860
So, "--" as we see is for file A.

05:10.860 --> 05:12.990
Plus is for file B.

05:12.990 --> 05:15.210
Anyway, what these numbers are telling us

05:15.210 --> 05:18.120
is how many lines have been extracted in this chunk.

05:18.120 --> 05:20.610
So, how big is this chunk from file A?

05:20.610 --> 05:23.550
And what part of the file do they start from?

05:23.550 --> 05:25.110
So, it's kind of confusing.

05:25.110 --> 05:26.100
You often don't,

05:26.100 --> 05:28.470
I personally don't spend a lot of time looking at this.

05:28.470 --> 05:30.270
I'm usually looking at the changes.

05:30.270 --> 05:32.940
But, just so you understand how to read it.

05:32.940 --> 05:36.330
This first bit says from file A, because the minus sign,

05:36.330 --> 05:39.480
four lines have been extracted starting from line three.

05:39.480 --> 05:42.450
So, this is not negative three, it's just three.

05:42.450 --> 05:44.280
The minus indicates A.

05:44.280 --> 05:46.440
So, four lines starting from line three.

05:46.440 --> 05:49.320
And so, the next part from file B,

05:49.320 --> 05:52.440
we have five lines starting from line three.

05:52.440 --> 05:54.450
So, I'm gonna diagram that out here.

05:54.450 --> 05:55.950
This is what our files looked like.

05:55.950 --> 05:57.900
This is file A, this is file B.

05:57.900 --> 05:59.790
Old version, from the last commits.

05:59.790 --> 06:01.350
New version, same file.

06:01.350 --> 06:03.060
But just what's changed.

06:03.060 --> 06:06.330
Well, the chunk says from file A,

06:06.330 --> 06:09.030
we get four lines starting from line three.

06:09.030 --> 06:09.900
So, 1, 2, 3.

06:09.900 --> 06:11.070
This is line three.

06:11.070 --> 06:11.910
We get four lines.

06:11.910 --> 06:13.590
Yellow, green, blue, purple.

06:13.590 --> 06:17.430
Then, file B is going to include five lines

06:17.430 --> 06:18.660
starting from line 3.

06:18.660 --> 06:19.890
1, 2, 3.

06:19.890 --> 06:22.200
So, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5.

06:22.200 --> 06:24.090
Now, one thing that is kind of confusing

06:24.090 --> 06:25.860
is that it shows orange right here.

06:25.860 --> 06:27.870
This is line two of our file.

06:27.870 --> 06:31.740
Technically, this is not really part of the chunk itself,

06:31.740 --> 06:34.230
depending on the language of your file.

06:34.230 --> 06:36.570
If it's Python or JavaScript or something else.

06:36.570 --> 06:39.660
Sometimes it'll display a little preview of a line.

06:39.660 --> 06:41.160
Other times, this is something else

06:41.160 --> 06:44.070
it's not reliably aligned from the chunk.

06:44.070 --> 06:44.903
It's confusing.

06:44.903 --> 06:45.933
So, it starts here.

06:46.770 --> 06:47.730
Anyway.

06:47.730 --> 06:50.070
So, what we have here are the two ranges, right?

06:50.070 --> 06:50.947
Those numbers,

06:50.947 --> 06:51.847
"3,4"

06:51.847 --> 06:53.280
"3,5"

06:53.280 --> 06:55.770
And then down below we have the actual changes.

06:55.770 --> 06:57.870
So, this is the content of the file,

06:57.870 --> 06:59.430
two files, superimposed

06:59.430 --> 07:03.030
where we can see every line that starts with a minus,

07:03.030 --> 07:05.550
it was a change that came from file A.

07:05.550 --> 07:09.330
And every line that starts with A plus comes from file B.

07:09.330 --> 07:13.230
So, these lines, yellow, green, blue are unchanged, right?

07:13.230 --> 07:14.670
They exist in both.

07:14.670 --> 07:17.610
But then purple, which is actually red,

07:17.610 --> 07:19.860
but purple has that minus sign,

07:19.860 --> 07:22.740
came from file A and it's not in file B.

07:22.740 --> 07:25.980
And then, indigo and violets came from file B.

07:25.980 --> 07:27.480
They're not in file A.

07:27.480 --> 07:30.600
Usually, Git picks A and B for us

07:30.600 --> 07:33.000
in such a way that you can think of file A

07:33.000 --> 07:35.700
as like the old stuff and file B as the new stuff.

07:35.700 --> 07:38.100
But, it doesn't always work out that way.

07:38.100 --> 07:40.350
So, this tells us as we already know,

07:40.350 --> 07:42.420
that yellow green and blue are in both files.

07:42.420 --> 07:43.830
Nothing changed there, right?

07:43.830 --> 07:45.510
Red, orange are both in there too.

07:45.510 --> 07:47.070
They're just not part of this chunk

07:47.070 --> 07:51.000
because it just picks a small little bit of context

07:51.000 --> 07:53.670
around a change, and the change happened right there.

07:53.670 --> 07:56.160
Purple turned to indigo and violet.

07:56.160 --> 07:59.130
So, indigo and violet are new, purple gone.

07:59.130 --> 08:01.192
So, here's a more complicated example.

08:01.192 --> 08:04.470
This is for a style sheet, "main.css".

08:04.470 --> 08:05.730
We can see up top,

08:05.730 --> 08:08.790
same file just compared A and B.

08:08.790 --> 08:10.260
Then we see the chunk header.

08:10.260 --> 08:12.450
And then here's our chunk, some context,

08:12.450 --> 08:15.660
and we see that we ended up with four new lines

08:15.660 --> 08:19.200
in this chunk, added, as part of file B.

08:19.200 --> 08:22.350
And then here, the second chunk in the same file,

08:22.350 --> 08:23.370
it just skips down.

08:23.370 --> 08:25.320
We're starting at what, line 63 now?

08:25.320 --> 08:26.850
Instead of 22.

08:26.850 --> 08:28.080
There's a whole bunch of stuff in between

08:28.080 --> 08:29.490
that just doesn't show up.

08:29.490 --> 08:33.780
We see that file A had these lines in red that are gone,

08:33.780 --> 08:35.550
and file B has these, what?

08:35.550 --> 08:37.200
Six lines that are new.

08:37.200 --> 08:39.330
So, that's a basic concept for how these diffs,

08:39.330 --> 08:41.070
these outputs work.

08:41.070 --> 08:42.090
If we just go back here,

08:42.090 --> 08:44.130
I know it's kind of crazy to look at.

08:44.130 --> 08:45.930
But hopefully, you can step through

08:45.930 --> 08:48.330
and understand what each line does.

08:48.330 --> 08:51.570
So, this is telling us we're comparing two files,

08:51.570 --> 08:54.630
same name just at different points in time.

08:54.630 --> 08:57.360
What those points in time are, we can set up ourself.

08:57.360 --> 08:59.610
We'll learn how to do that in the next couple of videos.

08:59.610 --> 09:01.710
And then we see the stuff, the Metadata,

09:01.710 --> 09:03.450
we really don't need to care about.

09:03.450 --> 09:07.530
Then we see each version A and B are both assigned a symbol.

09:07.530 --> 09:10.260
A gets minus, B gets plus.

09:10.260 --> 09:11.550
Then we have the chunk header.

09:11.550 --> 09:15.810
It tells us seven lines from line 25 are coming from file A,

09:15.810 --> 09:20.100
and seven lines starting at line 25 are coming from file B.

09:20.100 --> 09:21.520
Then, what we see here

09:21.520 --> 09:23.730
are those seven lines from file A.

09:23.730 --> 09:24.563
Right?

09:24.563 --> 09:25.500
1, 2, 3.

09:25.500 --> 09:26.940
This one came from A.

09:26.940 --> 09:29.220
So, 4, 5, 6, 7.

09:29.220 --> 09:30.930
And the seven lines from file B.

09:30.930 --> 09:33.780
1, 2, 3, skip that one.

09:33.780 --> 09:36.300
4, 5, 6, 7.

09:36.300 --> 09:38.640
And then, of course the color coding is helpful

09:38.640 --> 09:42.480
but this minus sign means it's a file A line

09:42.480 --> 09:44.070
that only exists in file A.

09:44.070 --> 09:46.230
This means it only exists in file B.

09:46.230 --> 09:48.783
Usually, this means old versus new.

09:49.860 --> 09:52.110
And then we've got a second chunk in the same file

09:52.110 --> 09:54.630
where there's just a line that I added "CHICKENS"

09:54.630 --> 09:56.160
that only exists in file B.

09:56.160 --> 10:00.390
Nothing was removed or changed in file A, at this point.

10:00.390 --> 10:01.890
And then we've got other files, right?

10:01.890 --> 10:04.890
The same sort of idea applies to this different file

10:04.890 --> 10:07.230
or to this JavaScript file.

10:07.230 --> 10:09.300
There are different chunks exerted,

10:09.300 --> 10:11.412
excerpt, excerpted?

10:11.412 --> 10:13.590
Excerpt, I don't know how you say that.

10:13.590 --> 10:16.470
Different excerpts from the files.

10:16.470 --> 10:17.910
All right, so in the next video we'll talk

10:17.910 --> 10:20.100
about different ways that we can use git diff,

10:20.100 --> 10:21.660
and to make different comparisons

10:21.660 --> 10:23.940
where we will see very similar looking outputs

10:23.940 --> 10:25.390
that follow the same pattern.
